WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.080
Dell has just updated their XPS 13 laptop,
so we figured we would compare it to a closely-priced

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.490
MacBook Pro, but unfortunately, for the second
year in a row, Apple has left the lower-end

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.320
13” model completely untouched, still packing
an old dual-core processor.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.980
And this is really disappointing since the
XPS packs a quad-core processor and the MacBook

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.600
Pro is quite a bit more expensive for a similarly
specced model.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.670
So we know that the Dell is gonna be a faster
machine for a cheaper price, but what if the

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:32.369
MacBook Pro will still give you a better overall
experience despite those downsides?

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.469
So much so that it’ll actually be worth
spending the extra cash?

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:39.440
For instance, the biggest factor in making
this decision is whether you like Windows

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.929
or macOS better.

00:00:40.929 --> 00:00:45.129
Some people can’t stand Windows, so they’ll
gladly pay extra cash for a Mac, but for those

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:47.969
who don’t really mind either one, let’s
get started.

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.699
Last year’s XPS 13 featured Windows Hello,
which automatically logged you into your laptop

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:55.129
by using cameras to recognize your face.

00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:57.999
It was great, but it required the cameras
to sit below the display, making for an unpleasant

00:00:57.999 --> 00:00:58.999
camera angle.

00:00:58.999 --> 00:01:02.979
Well this year, that feature is gone, so the
webcam goes back up above the display without

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:04.910
affecting the top bezel much at all.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.280
The MacBook Pro clearly has larger bezels
that we wish Apple would slim down already,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.670
but it still looks okay overall.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.431
Technically, both displays are 13.3” large,
but it turns out that MacBook Pro’s display

00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:21.690
actually has around 4 square inches more screen
space due to using a 16:10 aspect ratio instead

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.950
of 16:9, which is great for watching fullscreen
16:9 videos, but the downside is that you

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.960
don’t get as much vertical space while browsing
the web, which is a huge positive for the

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.960
MacBook.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.570
Not only that, but the MacBook Pro gets a
2.5K resolution display compared to this XPS

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.170
which is packing 1080p, and the difference
is easily noticeable.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.850
The XPS also has a 4K version but it’s a
few hundred dollars more expensive and more

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.660
comparable to the Touch Bar MacBook Pro.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:51.330
We also tested screen brightness and the MacBook
definitely gets a lot brighter, which is really

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.830
nice for using in bright rooms or outside,
and it’s also much more color accurate,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.500
with P3 wide-color gamut.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.000
As for the design itself, the MacBook’s
shell is machined out of a solid block of

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.580
aluminum, so it feels great.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.220
The XPS, on the other hand, uses a lot of
plastic sandwiched between an aluminum case,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.291
and it’s also quite a bit thicker than the
MacBook Pro at their thickest points, but

00:02:12.291 --> 00:02:16.200
slightly thinner at their thinnest points,
and when you stack them on top of each other,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.950
it’s also quite a bit smaller.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:23.430
For ports, the XPS features three gen-2 USB-C
ports, with two of them supporting Thunderbolt

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.120
3, alongside a headphone jack and a mini-SD
card slot.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.040
The MacBook is limited to just two Thunderbolt
3 ports and a headphone jack, so the XPS wins

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.040
there.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.250
They both use USB-C for charging, but the
XPS’s charging cable is built into the power

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.819
brick, so you can’t disconnect it and use
it for data transfer like you can with the

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:41.980
MacBook Pro.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.680
Just like last year, the XPS is a bit tough
to open up since it lacks a notch in the front,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.490
and the hinge has a lot of resistance, while
the MacBook Pro easily opens up with one finger.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:56.180
The XPS’s power button doubles as a fingerprint
reader so you can conveniently login without

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.400
typing in the password like you have to do
on the MacBook, and the keyboard feels pretty

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.099
great with a lot of key travel.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:05.000
The MacBook Pros keys are very clicky, which
I personally prefer, but it’s definitely

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.459
not as comfortable to type on with its low
key travel.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:11.780
And perhaps the biggest downside for the MacBook
is that it uses the failure-prone butterfly

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.060
keyboard that’s been causing Apple a lot
of issues for recent years.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.740
Now for the trackpads, the MacBook Pro has
the best trackpad out of any laptop ever,

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.760
and I’m not exaggerating.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.130
It doesn’t actually move or click, it uses
force-touch technology to simulate clicks

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.349
using haptic feedback, so it’s extremely
reliable and you get an even clicking feel

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:30.349
all-around.

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:34.170
And you can even customize the force it takes
to register a click in the settings.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.211
The XPS’s trackpad is much smaller so you
don’t get as much room to move around, and

00:03:38.211 --> 00:03:41.810
it requires more pressure to click the higher
you get on the trackpad.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.690
However, it’s pretty decent compared to
other Windows laptops.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.319
For the webcams, neither look very great at
all, with the MacBook having a lot of visible

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:54.340
noise, which the XPS gives rid of using some
heavy noise reduction, but it also adds quite

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:04.000
a lot of saturation, making skin look a bit
orange.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.890
But as you just heard in that clip, the microphone
quality on the Dell is horrible compared to

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:08.890
the MacBook.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:34.150
And before we get into performance, let’s
compare the speaker quality.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:38.241
As you’ve heard, the MacBook Pro blows the
XPS’s speakers out of the water.

00:04:38.241 --> 00:04:42.550
There really is no comparison in everything
from volume to dynamic range and just overall

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.190
sound quality.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:48.710
So far, the MacBook Pro is looking like the
champ in overall user experience, from the

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:55.120
design, build quality, trackpad, speakers
and display quality, even despite the subpar

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.120
keyboard.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:01.160
However, the XPS packs a quad-core processor
while the MacBook only gets two cores, so

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.590
let’s get into performance, starting with
Geekbench 4’s multi-core test.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:08.830
For some reason, the Dell is scoring barely
any faster than the MacBook, which isn’t

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:09.980
right at all.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.280
It turns out that you have to click the battery
icon in the system tray and switch the battery

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.610
saving slider to best performance.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:19.920
And you also have to plug in power cable to
unlock all of the performance.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.410
Now we’re getting a much higher score that
completely destroys the MacBook Pro, but we’re

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.040
also gonna see less battery life than what
Dell rates it at.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.780
We saw similar results in the more realistic
Cinebench R15 benchmark, where we got almost

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:36.260
100 more points by plugging it into power
and using best performance mode.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:40.020
But either way, the XPS outperforms the MacBook
Pro by a massive margin.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.220
And in Geekbench 4’s graphics test, the
XPS is once again scoring better than the

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.210
MacBook Pro, so we’re really impressed with
its performance.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.970
But on a positive note, the MacBook Pro’s
SSD storage is quite a bit faster than the

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.060
Dells, which is useful for everything from
transferring data to opening apps.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.639
But the Dell is the clear winner in overall
performance, so if you’re using professional

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:02.611
apps that can use that power, you should definitely
choose the Dell, but if you just need a powerful

00:06:02.611 --> 00:06:07.139
laptop to do basic things like browsing the
internet, using web-based apps and basic stuff

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:10.210
like that, the MacBook will be more than powerful
enough.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.920
But then again, if you’re not using professional
apps, you really don’t need the MacBook

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:14.920
Pro.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.330
The better option is the 2018 MacBook Air.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:22.850
It’s got newer technology with the T2 chip,
fingerprint reader, a more comfortable keyboard,

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.009
better battery life and a better price.

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:28.050
So in this case, who is this 2017 MacBook
Pro for?

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.270
Well, it’s for those who are using pro apps
that are exclusive to macOS.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:38.580
But even in this case, I would seriously recommend
just paying $1800 for the 2019 MacBook Pro,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:52.630
which just came out last month and has a quad-core
processor.

